After trying, I found the things got tricky for dealing with extensions.
I have an implementation of `tryResolvePostfixedFile`

```ts
function tryResolvePostfixedFile(
  file: string,
  postfix: string,
  options: InternalResolveOptions,
  tryIndex: boolean,
  targetWeb: boolean,
  preserveSymlinks: boolean,
  tryPrefix?: string,
  skipPackageJson?: boolean
): string | undefined {
  let res: string | undefined

  // if we fould postfix exist, we should first try resolving file with postfix. details see #4703.
  if (
    postfix &&
    (res = tryResolveFile(
      `${file}/${postfix}`,
      '',
      options,
      tryIndex,
      targetWeb,
      preserveSymlinks,
      tryPrefix,
      skipPackageJson,
    ))
  ) {
    return res
  }

  if (
    (res = tryResolveFile(
      file,
      postfix,
      options,
      tryIndex,
      targetWeb,
      preserveSymlinks,
      tryPrefix,
      skipPackageJson,
    ))
  ) {
    return res
  }
}
```

It doesn't work well with
```ts
for (const ext of options.extensions || DEFAULT_EXTENSIONS) {
    if (
      (res = tryResolvePostfixedFile(
        file + ext,
        postfix,
        options,
        false,
        targetWeb,
        preserveSymlinks,
        options.tryPrefix,
        options.skipPackageJson
      ))
    ) {
      return res
    }
  }
```

The position of `+ ext` seems improper for both `file + ext` and `postfix + ext`.
